## Proposed Solutions

### Solution 1: Segment Production Health Check

Add monitoring to detect when segments stop being produced:

```typescript
// In monitor.ts or new dedicated module
let lastSegmentTime = Date.now();
let lastSegmentIndex = 0;

// On each segment produced (in fmp4Segmenter.ts)
function onSegmentProduced(index: number): void {
  lastSegmentTime = Date.now();
  lastSegmentIndex = index;
}

// In monitor interval
const segmentAge = Date.now() - lastSegmentTime;
if (segmentAge > 10000) { // No segment for 10 seconds
  // Recovery reported success but segments stopped
  // Escalate to higher recovery level
}
```

### Solution 2: FFmpeg Output Monitoring

Track FFmpeg stdout activity:

```typescript
let lastFFmpegOutput = Date.now();

ffmpeg.stdout.on("data", () => {
  lastFFmpegOutput = Date.now();
});

// Check periodically
if (Date.now() - lastFFmpegOutput > 5000) {
  // FFmpeg pipeline stalled
}
```

### Solution 3: Recovery Validation

After recovery completes, verify segments are actually flowing before declaring success:

```typescript
async function validateRecovery(segmenterBefore: number): Promise<boolean> {
  await delay(3000); // Wait for pipeline to produce
  const segmenterAfter = getSegmentIndex();
  return segmenterAfter > segmenterBefore;
}
```

